{"author":{"id":"b409816c5cd5ab140d1d52eb33aace35de44952bb794b19806fd57f6ecb1ace9"},"ops":[{"type":3,"timestamp":1791283983,"nonce":"ptPqmzrFwWFalahEb/U95a5FBpE=","message":"Fresh NUC screening after 5f8dc2fa5e3a6001af794fdc2d647e872fabf704, 2026-10-06. Intel i5-1250P x86_64; isolated source checkout, same locked dependencies as the Mac. LLVM enabled and checked by the harness. PyPy 7.3.20 / Python 3.11.13. Other user workloads were left running.\n\nFull --fast suite: five scaled trials, one for expensive kernels. These are mean timed-trial and whole-process wall ms, not a 50-trial steady-state verdict. Compile and tier-up may be inside the measured trials. All 22 supported checksum checks pass; pidigits remains unsupported because ints are 64 bits.\n\n| Kernel | zypy mean ms | PyPy mean ms | zypy wall ms | PyPy wall ms |\n|---|---:|---:|---:|---:|\n| ai | 43.47 | 18.34 | 463.50 | 317.28 |\n| chaos | 30.14 | 18.57 | 535.74 | 215.82 |\n| crypto_pyaes | 26.90 | 32.46 | 255.70 | 212.36 |\n| deltablue | 28.49 | 11.39 | 268.47 | 95.89 |\n| fannkuch | 36.39 | 55.66 | 246.26 | 311.60 |\n| float | 9.66 | 10.23 | 122.20 | 90.29 |\n| go | 16.36 | 91.49 | 472.78 | 1504.68 |\n| hexiom2 | 2618.24 | 4969.09 | 2775.72 | 5014.90 |\n| json_bench | 476.20 | 2296.38 | 2472.17 | 11529.00 |\n| meteor-contest | 62.77 | 41.27 | 565.66 | 270.42 |\n| nbody_modified | 7.84 | 7.60 | 148.58 | 71.87 |\n| nqueens | 5204.70 | 6383.86 | 5476.93 | 6439.35 |\n| pyflate-fast | 211.66 | 230.01 | 2600.82 | 2619.38 |\n| raytrace-simple | 91.94 | 8.62 | 1292.51 | 284.42 |\n| richards | 1.42 | 1.66 | 137.07 | 96.30 |\n| scimark_fft | 342.92 | 114.16 | 536.86 | 149.82 |\n| scimark_lu | 437.13 | 144.56 | 629.88 | 182.55 |\n| scimark_montecarlo | 52.64 | 128.99 | 243.65 | 171.04 |\n| scimark_sor | 34376.43 | 201.02 | 34573.16 | 239.09 |\n| scimark_sparsematmult | 184.62 | 129.35 | 377.33 | 165.72 |\n| spectral-norm | 49.89 | 6.64 | 335.66 | 68.73 |\n| telco | 16.91 | 21.84 | 313.93 | 306.93 |\n\n11/22 kernel wins vs PyPy, 6/22 process wall wins. The earlier d04fb58c screen was 9/22 and 5/22; crypto_pyaes/pyflate-fast crossed on this run and pyflate-fast wall is almost tied. Do not attribute these crossings to the promotion fix: its five-round interleaved A/B ranges mostly overlap and show no broad speedup. Strongly favorable kernels remain fannkuch, go, hexiom2, JSON and nqueens.\n\nLargest remaining kernel gaps: SOR 171x, raytrace-simple 10.7x, spectral-norm 7.5x, FFT/LU approximately 3x. Startup still loses on most short kernels.\n\nFresh LLVM-enabled type trace of the shipped scimark_sor check confirms the Array2D specialization gap: get/set keys stay Object, _idx(x:Object,y:Object)-\u003eObject, while SOR_execute has a G:Class(Array2D) instance and ArrayList has Tuple(Int,Int)-\u003eFloat indexing. This remains tracked in c255835 and 054aec3bc2e926f8bf0cc763c2263b0b2f58af188d5bb72f4133cd732d7650ec. Per-signature dispatch for overridden methods and a guarded entry from the dynamic receiver are needed; changing thresholds cannot supply the missing typed body.\n\nThe shared saved-stub promotion bug is closed by 5f8dc2fa5e3a6001af794fdc2d647e872fabf704 under git-bug 42962510dd69dc0edd41a56171a19cde70ad1f3d601a638645e1a1c0da3321f5. 17 compiler promotion/OSR tests pass on Mac and NUC; 43 Lua and 50 Python conformance groups pass on Mac with LLVM and both worker modes.\n\nRaw NUC artifacts: /tmp/zypy-nuc-promoted-stub-final.json and .log; baseline /tmp/zypy-nuc-d04-baseline.json; interleaved A/B /tmp/zyntax-nuc-promotion-ab.json. Mac --fast results /tmp/zypy-mac-promoted-stub.json also pass all 22 supported checks; timings differ by architecture.","files":null}]}